review.raw:
VERDICT
Partly correct: the logic works, but looping through the range takes linear time O(n) which results in Time Limit Exceeded (TLE) when the interval is up to 10^9.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You initialized your counter properly at 0.

You checked every number in the range using an inclusive loop (i <= high).

You used the modulo operator (i % 2 != 0) to correctly identify odd numbers.

Is it correct?

It is logically correct on small inputs, but it fails on large ranges due to Time Limit Exceeded (TLE).

Failing test case: low = 1, high = 1000000000 (10^9).

Line causing failure:

C++
for(int i=low; i<=high; i++){
Fix:

C++
return (high + 1) / 2 - low / 2;
Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

No arithmetic bug exists for small numbers, but iterating up to 10^9 times takes several seconds, exceeding LeetCode's typical 1-2 second execution limit.

Edge cases handled properly by logic: low == high (single number), both odd, both even, and alternating ends.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(high - low + 1) because the for-loop visits every single number between low and high one by one.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because only two scalar variables (count and i) are used.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't:

It works mathematically because it directly checks each number, but it fails in execution because high can reach 10^9, which requires one billion loop steps.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Linear Loop Simulation (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

Standard beginner brute force baseline.

Idea:

Walk from low to high one number at a time, check if each number has a remainder of 1 when divided by 2, and increment a counter.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countOdds(int low, int high) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = low; i <= high; i++) {
            if (i % 2 != 0) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(high - low) linear time scanning every number.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

Half of all consecutive numbers are odd. We can count the total numbers in the range and adjust by 1 if both ends are odd, solving it in O(1) time without any loop.

Approach 2: Interval Length and Boundary Parity Check

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 2 and standard counting math.

Idea:

The total count of numbers in the range is (high - low + 1). Exactly half of these numbers are odd, except when both low and high are odd, which gives one extra odd number.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countOdds(int low, int high) {
        if (low % 2 != 0 && high % 2 != 0) {
            return (high - low) / 2 + 1;
        }
        return (high - low + 1) / 2;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) because it performs basic arithmetic and comparisons in constant time.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of checking both boundaries with if-else conditions, we can count total odd numbers from 1 to high, subtract odd numbers from 1 to low - 1, which simplifies to a clean one-line formula (prefix counting).

Approach 3: Prefix Range Subtraction (Prefix Math)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 1 and top community solutions.

Idea:

The count of odd numbers between 1 and n is (n + 1) / 2. The odds between low and high is simply the odds up to high minus the odds up to (low - 1): ((high + 1) / 2) - (low / 2).

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countOdds(int low, int high) {
        return (high + 1) / 2 - low / 2;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) performing two divisions and one subtraction.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

We can express the same logic using bitwise operators instead of arithmetic operators, replacing + 1 and / 2 with bitwise shifts and bit checks.

Approach 4: Bitwise Shift and Masking

Where it comes from:

Competitive programming bit-manipulation optimizations.

Idea:

Dividing by 2 is equivalent to right-shifting by 1 (x >> 1). Adding 1 if low is odd can be checked using (low & 1). We can compute ((high - low) >> 1) + (low & 1 || high & 1).

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int countOdds(int low, int high) {
        return ((high - low) >> 1) + ((low & 1) | (high & 1));
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(1) in a few CPU clock cycles.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 1 (Linear Loop Simulation).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers expect Approach 2 or Approach 3. They want to see that you recognize this as an O(1) math problem rather than an O(n) loop problem.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: How does the prefix formula (high + 1) / 2 - low / 2 work for small numbers like low = 3, high = 7?

Answer: Odds up to 7 are (7 + 1) / 2 = 4 (which are 1, 3, 5, 7). Odds before 3 are 2 / 2 = 1 (which is 1). Subtracting them gives 4 - 1 = 3 (the odds 3, 5, 7).

Question 2: Can (high + 1) overflow a 32-bit signed integer?

Answer: LeetCode constraints state high is up to 10^9, while signed 32-bit INT_MAX is roughly 2 * 10^9 (2147483647). Thus high + 1 cannot overflow. If high could equal INT_MAX, we would rewrite it as high / 2 - low / 2 + (high % 2 != 0 || low % 2 != 0).

Question 3: How would you count numbers divisible by an arbitrary integer k in the range [low, high]?

Answer: Use prefix counting: countDivisible(high, k) - countDivisible(low - 1, k), which evaluates to (high / k) - ((low - 1) / k).

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Counting items with regular patterns in a range is an O(1) math problem solved by counting up to high and subtracting what comes before low.

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Check constraints first: if high can be 10^9, an O(n) loop will always trigger Time Limit Exceeded.

Look for regular repetitions: odd and even numbers strictly alternate every 2 steps.

Use prefix subtraction: f(low, high) = f(0, high) - f(0, low - 1).

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

Prefix Counting and Range Arithmetic in O(1).

THE BEST SOLUTION

